general_comments.body:
@triqi thank you for working on this!

I think the API should be

``` javascript
roundTo(15, 'minutes');
roundTo(15, 'minutes', {mode: 'floor'|'ceil'|'round'});
```

First the ordering -- the new style for add/subtract methods is value first, unit second `add(5, 'minutes')` reads better. About the up/down -- we might decide to add more settings in the future, like control over anchoring (where to set the 0 of the rounding, `now` is a viable option), or rounding to the end of the interval (like `endOf`), so making it an object is more extensible.

Also there are 3 ways of rounding -- down, up and just round, and these are better known as floor, ceil and round. So it makes sense to reuse those names. We might have up/down/nearest as a new-age equivalents :) There are actually more modes -- depending on if the moment is in the past/future -- round towards now or towards infinity. I guess we can leave those out for now, just mentioning for completeness.

@icambron what do you think?

After reading the code, it looks like it will do this (after reordering the arguments as suggested above):

``` javascript
moment("2014-01-01T12:34:56.7890").roundTo(15, "minutes").toISOString();
    // "2014-01-01T12:30:56.7890"
```

That doesn't match my intuitions for a `roundTo` method at all; I feel like all the smaller units should be zeroed out after rounding to the unit of interest.  (Well, technically we should send them to their minimum value -- days should become 1 when rounding to months or years.)

might I suggest the following?

call the following to either:-
roundedTime = moment.roundMoment(myMoment, 'hour', 3, 'down', true);//this will give you the rounded time without changing your original time

or

myMoment.roundTo('hour', 3, 'down'); //this will round the time inside myMoment

code as follows:- (I stole the switch/case from startOf and changed it a bit to marry up with .set)

```
moment.roundMoment = function (m, unit, offset, midpoint, clone) {
    unit = moment.normalizeUnits(unit);

    if (unit.toLowerCase() == 'day')
        unit = 'date';

    offset = offset || 1;
    var value = m.get(unit);

    switch (midpoint) {
        case 'up':
            value = Math.ceil(value / offset);
            break;
        case 'down':
            value = Math.floor(value / offset);
            break;
        case 'nearest':
        default:
            value = Math.round(value / offset);
            break;
    }
    var ret = clone ? m.clone() : m;
    ret = ret.set(unit, value * offset);

    switch (unit) {
        case 'year':
            ret.month(0);
            /* falls through */
        case 'month':
            ret.date(1);
            /* falls through */
        case 'date':
            ret.hours(0);
            /* falls through */
        case 'hour':
            ret.minutes(0);
            /* falls through */
        case 'minute':
            ret.seconds(0);
            /* falls through */
        case 'second':
            ret.milliseconds(0);
            /* falls through */
    }
    return ret;
}
moment.fn.roundTo = function (unit, offset, midpoint) {
    return moment.roundMoment(this, unit, offset, midpoint, false);
};
```

pr_reviewer_data.description:
This pull request will improve the performance as I am pre calculating the array length before the for loop execution
For example
```
let i, arr = [1,2,3,4,5];
for(i = 0; i< arr.length; i++) {
    console.log(i)
}
```
arr.length will execute in each iteration which slows our execution. For this I just pre calculate the length and use that variable in each iteration like
```
let i, arr = [1,2,3,4,5], arrLen = arr.length;
for(i = 0; i< arrLen; i++) {
    console.log(i)
}
```

I am also attachig the test suite case run time which is decrease by 610ms after changes

